@reporter, for context, the first 3 cases passes instantly and only the last one takes 5s. From my understanding, it keeps trying to create an ElasticSearch client by making HTTP connection to a failed mock server until timing out (default at 5s). Since we expect that case to fail anyway but it still tries to establish an HTTP connection, should we reduce the time out for it? What am I missing here?

<img width="1879" height="1230" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/954db03a-3e93-47df-bd9e-5ee661f70b5f" />

From: `internal/storage/metricstore/elasticsearch/factory_test.go:TestNewFactory()`

I'm assuming it's because `mockServer` has been defined with a valid response and 200 status code. The last test case is suppose to create a new mock server with 'error' status. Sorry, I just did a quick debugging and found out that the `http://localhost:9090` is irrelevant since it is replaced by the new mock server anyway. I think there is an opportunity to refactor this so we can run them in parallel. What do you think?

```Go
func TestNewFactory(t *testing.T) {
	mockServer := setupMockServer(t, mockESServerResponse, http.StatusOK)
	...
	tests := []struct {
		name        string
		cfg         config.Configuration
		response    []byte
		statusCode  int
		expectedErr bool
	}{
		...
		{
			name:        "ping failure for version detection",          // New situation to test error from create es client
			cfg:         newTestFactoryConfig("http://localhost:9090"), // Overridden by mock server
			response:    []byte(`{"error": "internal server error"}`),
			statusCode:  http.StatusInternalServerError,
			expectedErr: true,
		},
	}
	for _, tt := range tests {
		t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			if tt.response != nil && tt.statusCode != 0 {
				server := setupMockServer(t, tt.response, tt.statusCode)
				tt.cfg.Servers = []string{server.URL}
			}
			f, err := NewFactory(context.Background(), tt.cfg, telemetry.NoopSettings(), nil)
			...
		})
	}
}
```

I notice that the `internal/tracegen/worker_test.go::Test_SimulateTraces()` takes 7 seconds every time because in the second case of the test table, it requires a 1-second pause for every trace. Is there a reason for using this unit instead of something smaller like `time.Milisecond`?

```golang
func Test_SimulateTraces(t *testing.T) {
	tests := []struct {
		name  string
		pause time.Duration
	}{
		...
		{
			name:  "with pause",
			pause: time.Second,
		},
	}

	for _, tt := range tests {
		t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			...
			worker := &worker{
				logger:  logger,
				tracers: tracers,
				wg:      &wg,
				id:      7,
				running: &running,
				Config: Config{
					Traces:     7,
					Duration:   time.Second,
					Pause:      tt.pause,
					Service:    "stdout",
					Debug:      true,
					Firehose:   true,
					ChildSpans: 1,
				},
			}
			expectedOutput := `{"level":"info","msg":"Worker 7 generated 7 traces"}` + "\n"
			worker.simulateTraces()
			assert.Equal(t, expectedOutput, buf.String())
		})
	}
}
```

@reporter , what do you think? Upon looking at the `config.go` in the same directory, `c.Duration` seems to have a default type of `time.Microsecond` on line 45. 

I don't see a reason for each simulating trace to pause for a whole second before finishing. Let me know if I'm overlooking something here. 

AFAIK, this is the longest test.
